Under `--modular-termination` together with `--decreases-orders`, a `decreases {:global}` or `decreases {:callback g}` bound may now contain a component `e by O`. The orders library is loaded under `--modular-termination` and verified with the program, not trusted. A bound must still not reach open dispatch. An order used across modules must therefore come from a `{:termination_closed}` function, and its well-foundedness proof must bind each chain with a quantifier. This follows the new _binder rule_: applying a variable of a total arrow type `A -> B` that is bound by a `forall` or `exists` expression, or by a ghost `forall` statement, is not a call through unknown code. Parameters, locals, fields, results and lambdas bound to variables are still unknown code. A program that does not use both options is unaffected. See https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/245.
